Q: cannot compile openssh due to OpenSSL library not found A: <p>The problem seems to come from <code>LD_LIBRARY_PATH</code> environment variable. Here come the test from <code>configure.ac</code> of <code>OpenSSH</code> source code :</p>
<pre><code>$ cat test.c
#include &lt;stdio.h&gt;
#include &lt;stdlib.h&gt;
#include &lt;string.h&gt;
#include &lt;openssl/opensslv.h&gt;
#include &lt;openssl/crypto.h&gt;
#define DATA &quot;conftest.ssllibver&quot;

int main() {
    FILE *fd;
    int rc;

    fd = fopen(DATA,&quot;w&quot;);
    if(fd == NULL)
        exit(1);
#ifndef OPENSSL_VERSION
# define OPENSSL_VERSION SSLEAY_VERSION
#endif
#ifndef HAVE_OPENSSL_VERSION
# define OpenSSL_version        SSLeay_version
#endif
#ifndef HAVE_OPENSSL_VERSION_NUM
# define OpenSSL_version_num    SSLeay
#endif
    if ((rc = fprintf(fd, &quot;%08lx (%s)\n&quot;,
        (unsigned long)OpenSSL_version_num(),
        OpenSSL_version(OPENSSL_VERSION))) &lt; 0)
        exit(1);

    exit(0);
}
$ export PKG_CONFIG_PATH=~/prog/opt/ssh/lib/pkgconfig/
$ gcc `pkg-config --cflags openssl` -c test.c
$ gcc -o test test.o `pkg-config --libs openssl`
$ ./test
./test: error while loading shared libraries: libcrypto.so.1.1: cannot open shared object file: No such file or directory
</code></pre>
<p>That is the reason why we get :</p>
<pre><code>checking OpenSSL library version... not found
configure: error: OpenSSL library not found.
</code></pre>
<p>When exporting <code>LD_LIBRARY_PATH</code> where your <code>libssl</code> libraries are living :</p>
<pre><code>$ export LD_LIBRARY_PATH=~/prog/opt/ssh/lib
$ ./test &amp;&amp; cat conftest.ssllibver
1010117f (OpenSSL 1.1.1w  11 Sep 2023)
</code></pre>
<p>You can then compile as usual :</p>
<pre><code>$ ./configure --prefix=$HOME/prog/opt/ssh/ --with-ssl-dir=$HOME/prog/opt/ssh/ --with-privsep-path=$HOME/prog
$ make
$ make install
$ PATH=~/prog/opt/ssh/bin:$PATH ssh -V
OpenSSH_8.4p1, OpenSSL 1.1.1w  11 Sep 2023
</code></pre>
<p>I am currently rescuing an old server running <code>OpenSSH_5.3p1</code> from 2009 ! Hopefully it works for you. Greetings.</p>

Q: How to remove the older projects entries/list/history from the Visual Studio Opening Window? A: <p>After trying to research this for the new Visual Studio 2026 without success, I decided to just figure it out myself.</p>
<p>I navigated to <code>%LocalAppData%\Microsoft\VisualStudio\18.0_somehash\</code> and initiated that entire directory in git and committed changes. This took a snapshot of the &quot;before&quot;. Opened up VS2026, manually deleted an entry, went back to git to see which files changed.</p>
<p>One was <code>UnifiedSettings\DefinitionCache.dat</code>, which unfortunately is a binary file, but it appears to be a cache db of some sorts, so probably safe to delete so I deleted that. The other was <code>ApplicationPrivateSettings.xml</code>, which fortunately for us is a text file.</p>
<p>Took the old version of the file, prettified it to make it readable, and looked at the diff. There appears to be two properties that store recent file paths:</p>
<ul>
<li><code>&lt;collection name=&quot;CodeContainers.Roaming&quot;&gt;</code></li>
<li><code>&lt;collection name=&quot;CodeContainers.Offline&quot;&gt;</code></li>
</ul>
<p>And within these is a json string that has a bunch of entries like this:</p>
<pre class="lang-json prettyprint-override"><code>  {
    &quot;Key&quot;: &quot;C:\\Path\\To\\Solution.slnx&quot;,
    &quot;Value&quot;: {
      &quot;LocalProperties&quot;: {
        &quot;FullPath&quot;: &quot;C:\\Path\\To\\Solution.slnx&quot;,
        &quot;Type&quot;: 0,
        &quot;SourceControl&quot;: null
      },
      &quot;Remote&quot;: null,
      &quot;IsFavorite&quot;: false,
      &quot;LastAccessed&quot;: &quot;2026-01-13T06:58:43.1506269+00:00&quot;,
      &quot;IsLocal&quot;: true,
      &quot;HasRemote&quot;: false,
      &quot;IsSourceControlled&quot;: false
    }
  }
</code></pre>
<p>This json string can be deleted if you want everything in that container removed, or you can also edit it yourself.</p>
<p>I did find that when the entire collection was deleted in VS via right-click &gt; delete, it didn't delete the entire key in this file, instead it looked like this:</p>
<h3>Empty Roaming:</h3>
<pre class="lang-json prettyprint-override"><code>        &lt;collection name=&quot;CodeContainers.Roaming&quot;&gt;
            &lt;value name=&quot;value&quot;&gt;[]&lt;/value&gt;
            &lt;value name=&quot;machineLocal&quot;&gt;False&lt;/value&gt;
        &lt;/collection&gt;
</code></pre>
<h3>Empty Local:</h3>
<pre class="lang-json prettyprint-override"><code>        &lt;collection name=&quot;CodeContainers.Offline&quot;&gt;
            &lt;value name=&quot;value&quot;&gt;[]&lt;/value&gt;
            &lt;value name=&quot;machineLocal&quot;&gt;True&lt;/value&gt;
        &lt;/collection&gt;
</code></pre>
<p>And then I was left with a nice blank &quot;Get Started&quot; screen! Didn't even ask me to sign back in.</p>
<p><a href="https://i.sstatic.net/7rVbOfeK.png" rel="nofollow noreferrer"><img src="https://i.sstatic.net/7rVbOfeK.png" alt="Visual Studio empty &quot;Get Started&quot; screen" /></a></p>
<p>I had a couple of corrupted entries in my list that I couldn't delete using the right-click &gt; delete, which is what prompted me to investigate this. Turns out the json was malformatted and had line breaks where there shouldn't have been.</p>
<p>By the way, the <code>.dat</code> file deletion did also wipe out my &quot;Recent Project Templates&quot;, so if that's important to you then maybe try just fixing the xml file first <em>(but my custom template I had stored was still there in the main list, so wasn't a complete reset)</em>. But those are easy to add back by just creating a new project using a template, so pretty minor.</p>
<p>Hope this helps!</p>
